    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>To protest property taxes in Harris County, file a protest of your appraised value with the Harris Central Appraisal District (HCAD) by May 15, or within 30 days of receiving your notice if that is later, then back it up with comparable sales, photos and repair estimates. Most homeowners can do the whole thing online through HCAD's iFile system, and many protests settle without ever sitting in front of the appraisal review board.</p>
<p>Here is how the process works step by step, what evidence actually moves the number, and what to do if the first answer is no.</p>

<h2>Why protest your Harris County appraisal</h2>
<p>Your tax bill equals your taxable value times the combined rate of every unit that taxes your home: school district, county, city, community college, MUD and others. You cannot protest the rates through HCAD, but you can protest the value, and every dollar of value you remove lowers the bill from every unit at once.</p>
<p>HCAD values homes across the county using mass appraisal models. Those models cannot see your cracked slab, original 1980s kitchen or the busy road behind your fence. A protest is your chance to show the appraiser what the model missed. Under Texas law, in a value protest the appraisal district carries the burden of proving its value by a preponderance of the evidence.</p>
<p>A protest works alongside your exemptions, not instead of them. If you have not filed for the <a href="/blog/texas-homestead-exemption/">Texas homestead exemption</a>, do that first. It removes $140,000 from your school taxable value in 2026 and caps future appraisal increases at 10% a year.</p>

<h2>Harris County protest deadlines</h2>
<p>For a homestead, HCAD is required to send the notice of appraised value by April 1 or as soon as practicable afterward. The key dates:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Protest deadline:</strong> May 15 or 30 days after the notice was delivered, whichever is later. A deadline on a weekend or holiday moves to the next business day.</li>
<li><strong>Late protests:</strong> If you miss the deadline, the appraisal review board (ARB) may still hear your protest if you file before it approves the appraisal records and show good cause. Do not count on it.</li>
<li><strong>Taxes due:</strong> Bills usually go out in the fall and become delinquent if not paid by January 31. Pay at least the undisputed amount on time, or you can lose the right to continue an appeal.</li>
</ul>
<p>Even if HCAD did not mail you a notice because your value did not rise, you can still protest. The notice deadline simply becomes May 15.</p>

<h2>How to file: HCAD iFile and iSettle</h2>
<p>HCAD's online system is the easiest way in. Look for your iFile number in the upper right corner of your notice of appraised value. It links your account to HCAD's online owner portal, where you can protest, receive electronic notices and use iSettle.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Create or sign in to your owner account</strong> and enter your iFile number.</li>
<li><strong>File the protest.</strong> Check both grounds that apply to most homeowners: value is over market value, and value is unequal compared with other properties.</li>
<li><strong>Enter your opinion of value</strong> and a short explanation. Be specific: "Three comparable sales within half a mile closed between $355,000 and $372,000; foundation repair estimate $14,500 attached."</li>
<li><strong>Opt in to iSettle and upload evidence.</strong> HCAD allows uploads only on accounts that opt into iSettle, and the window to upload after filing is short, so have your files ready before you start.</li>
<li><strong>Review the offer.</strong> An HCAD appraiser reviews sales and equity data plus your evidence and may send a settlement offer. You can accept it or reject it and move on to a meeting or hearing.</li>
</ol>
<p>You can also file a protest by mail or in person, or through HCAD's mobile app. The protest itself is free.</p>

<h2>Evidence that wins property tax protests</h2>
<p>Good evidence is about January 1 of the tax year. That is the valuation date for the whole year, so condition and sales near that date matter most.</p>
<h3>Comparable sales</h3>
<p>Comparables are the backbone of a market value protest. Look for three to six homes that:</p>
<ul>
<li>Sold within about six months before or after January 1</li>
<li>Sit in the same subdivision or within roughly a mile</li>
<li>Have similar square footage, age, lot size and condition</li>
<li>Were normal sales, not foreclosures or family transfers, unless those are common in your area</li>
</ul>
<p>Texas is a non-disclosure state, so sale prices are not public record. A Realtor can pull MLS sales for you, and HCAD makes the sales data it relied on available as hearing evidence once an offer is made or a meeting is scheduled.</p>
<h3>Equity (unequal appraisal) comparables</h3>
<p>An unequal appraisal argument says your home is valued higher than similar homes nearby, regardless of sales. Pull five to ten neighbors with similar size and age from HCAD's property search and compare value per square foot. This works well in neighborhoods with few recent sales.</p>
<h3>Condition and repair evidence</h3>
<ul>
<li>Dated photos of foundation cracks, roof damage, water stains, worn finishes or storm damage not yet repaired</li>
<li>Written contractor bids for needed repairs</li>
<li>An inspection report from your purchase, if it is recent</li>
<li>Your closing disclosure, if you bought near January 1 at a lower price than HCAD's value</li>
<li>Corrections to HCAD's record, such as wrong square footage or a pool you do not have</li>
</ul>
[[product:laser-measure]]
<p>A certified appraisal can also help. For a home valued at $1 million or less, if you deliver a qualifying appraisal by a Texas-certified appraiser to the chief appraiser at least 14 days before the hearing, the district must prove its value by clear and convincing evidence, a higher bar.</p>

<h2>Informal meeting vs. ARB hearing</h2>
<p>If iSettle does not resolve it, there are two more steps. You will usually get one notice for an informal meeting with an HCAD appraiser and, if needed, a second for a formal hearing before the appraisal review board.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th></th><th>Informal meeting</th><th>Formal ARB hearing</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Who you meet</td><td>An HCAD appraiser</td><td>A panel of ARB members, independent of HCAD</td></tr>
<tr><td>Required?</td><td>No, it is optional</td><td>Yes, if you have not settled</td></tr>
<tr><td>Outcome</td><td>A settlement offer you can accept or refuse</td><td>A binding order setting your value for the year</td></tr>
<tr><td>Format</td><td>In person or remote</td><td>In person, by phone or video, or by sworn affidavit</td></tr>
<tr><td>Typical length</td><td>Short, often 15 minutes or less</td><td>Short, often 15 minutes or less</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>At least 14 days before an ARB hearing, the chief appraiser must tell you that you can request, free of charge, the data and information HCAD will use at the hearing. Request it and look for weak comparables you can attack. Bring several printed copies of your own evidence, keep your presentation to a few minutes, and stick to numbers. HCAD offers some Saturday hearings in June and July if weekdays are hard.</p>

<h2>If you lose: arbitration or district court</h2>
<p>An ARB order is not the end of the road. Homeowners have two main options, and choosing one generally rules out the other for that year's value.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Regular binding arbitration.</strong> File a request with the Texas Comptroller within 60 days of receiving the ARB order. For a homestead there is no value limit. The deposit is $450 for a homestead valued at $500,000 or less and $500 above that. If the arbitrator's value is closer to yours than to the ARB's, you get the deposit back minus a $50 administrative fee. Details are on the <a href="https://comptroller.texas.gov/taxes/property-tax/arbitration/">Comptroller's arbitration page</a>.</li>
<li><strong>District court.</strong> File a petition for review within 60 days of receiving notice of the final order. This costs more and often involves an attorney, but it can make sense for larger disputes.</li>
</ul>
<p>For either route, your taxes must not be delinquent. Pay at least the undisputed amount before the delinquency date to keep your appeal alive. Rules and deposits can change, so confirm current requirements with the Comptroller or a property tax attorney before you file.</p>

<h2>Tips from Houston protests</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Protest every year your value looks high.</strong> It is free, and with a homestead cap a lower base value carries forward.</li>
<li><strong>Check your record first.</strong> Errors in square footage, quality grade or features are the fastest wins.</li>
<li><strong>Document storm and flood damage.</strong> If Harvey-style flooding, hail or a freeze damaged your home and it was not repaired by January 1, that affects value.</li>
<li><strong>Be calm and brief.</strong> Panels respond to evidence, not frustration about tax rates.</li>
<li><strong>Keep a folder.</strong> Save each year's notice, evidence and result. Next year's protest takes half the time.</li>
</ul>
<p>Before you file, estimate what a lower value would save with our <a href="/tools/texas-property-tax-calculator/">Texas property tax calculator</a>. It helps you decide whether to accept an offer or push on to a hearing.</p>

<h2>Bottom line</h2>
<p>Protesting property taxes in Harris County is free, mostly online and worth doing whenever your HCAD value looks higher than what your home would sell for on January 1. File by May 15 or 30 days after your notice, lead with solid comparable sales and repair evidence, take a fair iSettle offer, and remember arbitration or district court if the ARB gets it wrong. For more ways to keep ownership costs down, see our <a href="/category/homeownership/">homeownership guides</a>.</p>
